replaceCostaRicaSection("costa-rica","settlement","<p>The <a href='costa-rican-civil-war.html'>Civil War of 1948</a> ended with negotiated protection of the <a href='social-guarantees-costa-rica.html'>Social Guarantees</a>, recognition of <a href='otilio-ulate-blanco.html'>Otilio Ulate's</a> election, and an eighteen-month <a href='founding-junta-second-republic.html'>Founding Junta</a> under <a href='jose-figueres-ferrer.html'>José Figueres Ferrer</a>.</p><p>The <a href='constitution-of-costa-rica-1949.html'>1949 Constitution</a> established independent electoral administration, strong autonomous public institutions, expanded citizenship, and the <a href='abolition-costa-rican-army.html'>abolition of the standing army</a>.</p>");
replaceCostaRicaSection("costa-rica","security","<p>The <a href='security-forces-costa-rica.html'>Security Forces of Costa Rica</a> combine an enlarged Civil Guard, police, border detachments, rural and maritime services, intelligence cooperation, and air surveillance. They can contain smuggling, protect institutions and negotiations, and police frontiers, but cannot independently resist a neighboring field army or great-power landing.</p><p>San José therefore couples neutrality to diplomacy, regional law, American assurances it prefers not to invoke, and the expectation that no rival bloc will demand a base worth provoking Washington.</p>");
appendCostaRicaSection("costa-rica",{id:"principal-figures",title:"Founders and political traditions",html:"<p><a href='rafael-angel-calderon-guardia.html'>Rafael Ángel Calderón Guardia</a>, <a href='manuel-mora-valverde.html'>Manuel Mora Valverde</a>, and Archbishop Víctor Manuel Sanabria created the social-reform alliance of the 1940s. <a href='teodoro-picado-michalski.html'>Teodoro Picado</a> inherited that coalition but could not resolve the 1948 succession.</p><p><a href='jose-figueres-ferrer.html'>José Figueres Ferrer</a> won the civil war, while <a href='otilio-ulate-blanco.html'>Otilio Ulate Blanco</a> supplied the electoral claim that bounded revolutionary rule. The Second Republic rests upon the coexistence of their once-hostile traditions.</p>"});
